A_136 - As for animal farming, I personally believe there's nothing wrong with raising animals. The right way to do business with animals (let's assume cattle in this case) isn't to slaughter them and sell their meat, but to collect their waste and use it in agriculture. They're valuable and have a very specific economic value, as they contribute to improving plant health and producing better-quality fruits and vegetables. Furthermore, once dead, these animals can actually be "processed" by a butcher, reusing the meat as food for carnivores (dogs, crocodiles, lions, sharks, etc.), and the bones can also be used to make agricultural fertilizer. Wheelbarrows can be used to collect the waste scattered across pastures to facilitate the work, and separate wheelbarrows can be used to collect the remains of the animal that died a natural death. In addition to this way of doing business, however, there is also the possibility of creating agritourism, or organizing parties taking advantage of the large spaces.